ache-2.0 | requests 2.32 |\n| MIT | left-pad 0.2 |\n| GPL-3.0 | somelib 1.1 |\n\n**Flagged: `somelib==1.1` is GPL-3.0. This is a problem for a closed-source product.**\n\n- GPL-3.0 is strong copyleft. If you distribute a product that links to or bundles `somelib`, the combined work generally has to be released under GPL-3.0. That means providing the full source to recipients, and it conflicts with keeping the product closed.\n- GPL-3.0 also has patent-license and anti-\"tivoization\" terms. These can cause extra trouble if you ship on devices.\n- It matters most if you distribute the software: shipping binaries, installers, an on-prem build, or a mobile or desktop app. If it only runs on your own servers as a hosted service, the GPL's distribution trigger usually isn't met. Check this with counsel, though, and note that AGPL would be different.\n\n**Fine to ship:**\n- `requests` (Apache-2.0): permissive. You need to include the license text and NOTICE file, and keep the copyright notices.\n- `left-pad` (MIT): permissive. You need to include the copyright and license notice.\n\n**Options for `somelib`:**\n1. Replace it with a permissively licensed alternative.\n2. Remove the dependency, or rewrite the small part you use.\n3. Ask the authors for a commercial or alternative license.\n4. Keep it only if it's used in a way that doesn't involve distribution, such as an internal service. Get legal sign-off first.\n\n**Limits of this scan:** it only reads the license labels in `deps.txt`. It doesn't cover transitive dependencies, so `requests` pulls in others that should be checked too. It also doesn't verify that the declared licenses match what the packages actually ship. Before release, run a full scan of the resolved dependency tree, for example with `pip-licenses`. I'm not a lawyer, so have counsel review the `somelib` decision.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}